---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { Cover } from '../site/Cover';
import LevelTrail from '../site/LevelTrail.astro';
import { entriesIn, href, levels } from '../lib/site';
---
<Docs title="Overview" description="The browser's own stylesheet, repaired to WCAG 2.2 AA. Astro, React and StyleX.">
  <Cover />
  <p>
    Normal UI keeps the browser's built-in look (system font on white, blue underlined links, grey buttons, black
    text) and repairs it wherever it falls short of WCAG 2.2 AA. Nothing is added for decoration.
  </p>
  <h2>How the docs are organised</h2>
  <p>
    By <a href="https://atomicdesign.bradfrost.com/chapter-2/">atomic design</a>: five levels, each built from the
    one before it. Every component page says what it is made of and where it is used.
  </p>
  <LevelTrail />
  <dl>
    {levels.map((l) => (
      <>
        <dt><a href={href(`/${l.id}/`)}>{l.label}</a> <small>({entriesIn(l.id).length})</small></dt>
        <dd>{l.intro}</dd>
      </>
    ))}
  </dl>
  <p>
    Under them sit the foundations: the <a href={href('/guidelines/')}>Guidelines</a> (principles, colour, type,
    rhythm, controls and states, ARIA coverage), the <a href={href('/tokens/')}>Tokens</a> every value comes from, and
    <a href={href('/typography/')}>Typography</a>, every text element as plain HTML renders it.
  </p>
  <h2>How the code is layered</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><code>tokens.json</code> is the source of every value. <code>pnpm tokens</code> writes <code>src/styles/tokens.css</code> (custom properties, both color schemes) and <code>src/tokens.stylex.ts</code> (StyleX constants pointing at them).</li>
    <li><code>src/styles/base.css</code> styles the elements themselves, in the <code>normal-ui</code> layer. Write plain semantic HTML and it already looks right.</li>
    <li><code>src/components</code> holds the React components. They add the wiring (labels, ARIA, keyboard) and style themselves with StyleX, whose atomic layers sit above the base layer.</li>
    <li><code>src/styles/patterns.css</code> is the same composite styling for hand-written HTML (<code>.pd-badge</code>, <code>[role=tab]</code>…). The templates and pages use it.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/themes/')}>Themes</a> restyle all of it from a few token values and element rules, switched with <code>data-theme</code>.</li>
  </ol>
</Docs>
